===Question #18===
{{Cquote2|What did you learn from Chambers of Xeric and Theatre of Blood that you used for the design of Tombs of Amascut?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=95t7YVRRkGo&t=2765s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Arcane:''' The biggest one was just wanting some sort of linear scaling difficulty. We didn’t want to release a raid that only catered to the high-end. We tried to include many more players and not just get the low-level players in. It’s very intimidating if you think back to trying to learn ToB. You want to try and join a group, and they’re saying 50KC wreck or Scythe wreck, and you ask, “how do I get my 50KCs?”. You have to learn with other learners.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' That can be a fun experience, but it’s very slow and very painful. It’s just very unapproachable for many people, so we wanted to have something that’s hard and gets more difficult - and we still want people to feel that challenge - but we don’t want it to be forced upon you, and we want it to be more gradual.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' That’s where invocations came from. We want to have a lot of optional difficulties, and we want to reward people for that difficulty. That’s the system we ended up with, and we wanted the ability to learn content to be a bit nicer, and that’s why wipes are optional, so you can go in with infinite wipes and just die to a boss over and over again.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I think I talked about it ages ago about how difficulty by nature isn’t inaccessible. The systems that let you learn the content makes something accessible, and I used the example of Elden Ring doing that well.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' Elden Ring is a very accessible game because you can walk straight back in every time you die, and it’s like you’re restarting completely. You’ve got all your potions and stats, and you can go back in and kill the boss again. It’s a challenging game, sure, but it’s very accessible, and people like the learning process of, “oh, I died. I’ve got to dodge left this time”.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' That’s what I wanted to approach here: it isn’t just spending 20 minutes making a group into 25 minutes getting to Verzik and making one mistake at P2 Verzik, die and then sit in that purgatory and have to do it all again. It’s such a slog.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' So we ended up with invocations, and I think it’s done a really good job. People are still pushing up high invocations, and the Ornament Kits and pet transmogs will push people to put on more invocations. The loot is very rewarding, with high invocations and raid levels. But even on the lower end, if people just want a chill raid, they can just turn a bunch of them off. They’ll get less loot but can have a bit more of a relaxing time. And I think that’s good.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Wipe mechanics was the biggest thing I took away from ToB. It made it much more punishing and inaccessible to players because it made learning hard. We went into ToA and said, “we’ve got to learn from that. We won’t succeed unless we learn from that”. We can’t afford to spend 12 months if it’s going to be super hard to get into for players. We wanted to get that right, and I feel we’ve nailed it!
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I feel like we’ve made it possible for players to learn. As well as this, one of the more challenging things for ToB was getting a group, and you couldn’t try it out solo. At least with this raid, you can go in solo, and you can learn. Even if you want to play in groups, you can go in solo and experience some of the bosses yourself - get to grips with it and even try out some of the other invocations for yourself, and then join a team!
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It removes the barriers. We didn’t not want to offer a raid that had difficulty, and we tried to remove the barriers that stopped you from getting to that raid with difficulty. I feel like we’ve done that well.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' At the same time, the other thing I’d say from the other raids is that I want each raid to be different! What’s the point of us adding a copy-paste raid and doing ToB2 where it’s the same format, you fight one boss after another, and the final boss with the same wipe mechanics and everything?
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I want each raid to feel unique. Chambers of Xeric feels very different from Theatre of Blood and Tombs of Amascut. But I realise that there are a lot of elements of each of those raids that people like.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Chambers of Xeric is the most different, for sure. To some degree, it’s super accessible because there’s no wipe system, and you can just brute force Olm at the end of any raid. You can die 100 times, but you will still beat that raid if you keep going. In that sense, it’s accessible compared to this, but I think ToA does better at helping you learn so you can do it competently rather than 100 deaths.
* '''Mod Halo:''' I distinctly remember a big, big sheet we were looking at where we were trying to figure out the difficulty for ToA. I remember putting each raid level should be on this graph, and since release, I think we’ve hit the mark on that with the difficulty and accessibility, as Arcane and Kieren mentioned.
* '''Mod Halo:''' I’ve got friends who’ve never even raided before, and they’ve picked up the raid after not even stepping foot in Chambers or ToB, which is crazy!

===Question #19===
{{Cquote2|What mechanics are you the happiest with across the whole raid?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=95t7YVRRkGo&t=3196s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Halo:''' We touched on it in a previous question, but what I’m happiest with personally is probably the invocation system and how it panned out. I can’t speak for Arcane, but a big worry for me pre-release was, “ah, players are going to do level 600 in the first week and then we’re going to have to add a whole load of invocations and rebalance”. I think we hit the mark on that.
* '''Mod Halo:''' Alongside that, all the boss mechanics. We playtested them internally and figured out these little strategies we’re now seeing players pick up. It’s pretty cool that the internal brainstorming we did is also what players think, which helped with the balancing! Happy with most mechanics, to be honest.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' If I want to be super specific, the final phase of the Wardens is probably my favourite part of the whole raid. If I could pick one mechanic, it would be the tiles and how you’ve got to constantly focus on the opposition from side to side to middle. That feels awesome, and it looks awesome.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We had different ideas in the original version that we had before the playtest - I’m not sure how much we’ve even talked about how this has changed through the player-testing experience. The playtesters gave excellent feedback throughout the whole raid. We were able to tweak and develop mechanics further into what it is today, which we are all proud of.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' The original version was a bit more chaotic and memory-focused, where the boss would indicate the next four tile locations, and you had to move. That had its pros, but it had its downsides as well. Where we landed, with the spirit of the idea still there, I think is fantastic.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' On a much higher level, invocations are awesome. It’s an exciting way to approach difficulty. But I say this: we’re only two weeks after launch. I’m interested in where invocations will be in six months, in a year? How are people going to play with the system at that point? Are the strategies going to shift, or will they settle with, “this is the meta everybody uses, this is the setup”?
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Maybe that’s fine, but I think that’s where we’ll get a sense of if invocations have worked to the maximum of what we thought they would. Based on preferences, I’d love to see some flexibility and different options in how people are setting up even a year from now.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I’m going to go for something small. Before the ToA launch, a big Reddit meme said, “oh, they’re just going to add a bunch of Jad mechanics. What is a Jad mechanic? Is Zulrah a Jad mechanic? Is Jad a Jad mechanic?”. That was a meme for about a week just talking about Jad attacks.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' In essence, it’s just a different way of making players swap their Prayer. There’s one where you fire a projectile, and the damage has already been calculated as soon as it’s fired. So it doesn’t matter if you change your Prayer, or it could be that you have until the projectile hits to pray, or maybe it reacts to the boss’ animation. Perhaps it’s done as a pattern. You can make the player do the same thing in many different ways, but the player interacts slightly differently.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I remember with Zebak, he used to fire at all the players, and I had a random idea of, “what if he fired one ball, and that ball turned into multiple balls?” and then we implemented it! It felt so good that he just fires the jug in the air, and it explodes and rains down on you.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' But it’s the same as everything else. It’s the same as the boss doing the animation, and you pray to what it is. It’s not different, but it just feels good. I enjoyed messing around with that. It wasn’t revolutionary, but we separated an extra step out of it and suddenly felt good and different.
* '''Mod Light:''' My favourite mechanic is the camel in the lobby. That’s my favourite part of the raid. Not exactly a mechanic, but it’s my favourite part!
* '''Mod Kieren:''' The other thing I’ll say is the interface for the invocations. It’s wonderful, and the way the icons light up is great!